**`ErrorBoundary.tsx`** — usage:

```tsx
// Default fallback
<ErrorBoundary>
  <MyComponent />
</ErrorBoundary>

// Custom fallback
<ErrorBoundary fallback={(error, retry) => (
  <div>
    <p>{error.message}</p>
    <button onClick={retry}>Try again</button>
  </div>
)}>
  <MyComponent />
</ErrorBoundary>
```

Key details:
- `getDerivedStateFromError` captures the error into state, triggering the fallback render
- `componentDidCatch` logs `message`, `stack`, and React's `componentStack` to the console
- `retry` resets state to `null`, causing React to re-render children — if the root cause is gone (e.g. a flaky data fetch), they recover
- `fallback` prop is optional; omit it to use the built-in UI
